---
layout: base.njk
wide: true
pagination:
  data: collections.mediumPages
  size: 1
  alias: medium
  addAllPagesToCollections: false
navView: shelf
permalink: "{{ medium.urls.shelf }}"
eleventyComputed:
  title: "{{ medium.label | capitalize }}"
---
{#- One shelf per medium (2026-10-25): /shelf/ is games, /shelf/<medium>/
    every other. From 2026-09-11 until then it was one shelf for everything
    with a category filter chip beside the sort; the media tabs replaced the
    filter. -#}
{% set items = medium.subjects | shelveSort | shelfRanks %}

<h1>{{ title }}</h1>

{#- Sort, in CSS. The radios carry no script: each tile ships a precomputed rank
    per axis (see shelfRanks) and the checked radio decides which one `order`
    reads. DOM order is status order, so the default view needs no rule at all
    and a reader with no CSS still gets the shelf in its intended sequence.

    The caveat worth knowing: `order` moves tiles visually but not in the DOM, so
    tab and screen-reader order stay as authored. That is why the default is the
    authored one — only the opt-in axes diverge, and a reader who never touches
    the control is never out of sync.

    Hidden below six items: a shelf that fits on one screen is not one you sort. -#}
{#- The wrapper is what :has() hangs off — the radios and the grid have to sit
    inside one element for the checked state to reach the tiles. -#}
<div class="shelf-sorter">
{% if items | length > 5 %}
<fieldset class="shelf-sort">
  <legend>sort</legend>
  <input type="radio" name="shelf-sort" id="sort-status" checked>
  <label for="sort-status">status</label>
  <input type="radio" name="shelf-sort" id="sort-az">
  <label for="sort-az">a–z</label>
  <input type="radio" name="shelf-sort" id="sort-recent">
  <label for="sort-recent">recent</label>
  <input type="radio" name="shelf-sort" id="sort-canon">
  <label for="sort-canon">canon</label>
  <input type="radio" name="shelf-sort" id="sort-rating">
  <label for="sort-rating">rating</label>
</fieldset>
{% endif %}
{% if items | length > 5 %}
{#- The scale, printed: the one link to the page that shows every rung in
    order. Sits with the controls because "rating" is the axis it
    explains, and a word under a cover is only half a scale on its own. -#}
<a class="shelf-key" href="/r/">what the ratings mean</a>
{% endif %}

{#- One card per game: the cover, then two cells hung directly under it —
    status, then rating — then the title. The COVER identifies: a reader
    hunting for one game scans box art far faster than titles. The CELLS say
    what happened and what he thought, in that order, in words. The title comes
    LAST so the cells sit at one height across every row: put the caption
    between and every wrapped title pushes its badges down a line, which was
    the ragged shelf this layout replaced (2026-09-04).

    Two cells stacked, never side by side: at six columns the tile is ~140px
    and a status word plus a rating word in Silkscreen never both fit on one
    line ("incomplete" + "disliked" is ~160px of glyphs). Full-width cells fit
    every word in both vocabularies with room to spare.

    The aurora banner that used to sit above the cover is gone. Its status
    half had been redundant since the badges arrived, and recency-by-
    brightness was the subtlest encoding of the least important axis — still
    reachable through the `recent` sort.

    Covers are SOURCED, and sourced means sometimes absent. A subject without
    one falls back to its generated tile, stretched by CSS to the cover's 2:3,
    so the original guarantee holds (no shelf goes blank waiting on an image)
    and a half-covered shelf still lays out as one even grid. -#}
<ul class="shelf-grid shelf-{{ medium.id }}">
  {% for item in items %}
  <li class="shelf-item st-{{ item.status }}{% if item.canon %} is-canon{% elif item.rating and item.rating.slug == "favorite" %} is-favorite{% endif %}" style="--o-az:{{ item.oAz }};--o-recent:{{ item.oRecent }};--o-canon:{{ item.oCanon }};--o-rating:{{ item.oRating }}">
    <a href="/s/{{ item.slug }}/">
      <span class="shelf-card">
        <span class="shelf-art">
          {% if item.coverUrl %}
          {#- A real <img>, unlike every sky on the site: the generated art is
              decoration and belongs in CSS, but a cover is content, it wants an
              alt, and a shelf that keeps growing wants lazy loading. -#}
          <img class="shelf-cover{% if item.coverBox %} is-box{% endif %}" src="{{ item.coverUrl }}" alt="{{ item.title }} cover art" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
          {% else %}
          <span class="sky" style="background-image:url({{ item.skyUrl }})" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          {% endif %}
        </span>
        {#- SPANs, not links: the whole tile is already one anchor, and an <a>
            inside an <a> is invalid — the parser closes the outer one early
            and the tile's layout comes apart. The rung's view is one click
            further, from the subject page. -#}
        <span class="cell badge b-{{ item.status }}">{{ item.status | statusWord(item.medium) }}</span>
        {#- The rating cell. Canon supersedes (ratingOf returns null for the
            ten) and takes the cell itself, gold with the ♥, so the top of the
            ladder is read in the same place as every other rung. The rung
            below wears silver on the whole card: metal orders the top two
            where English couldn't order "favorite" against "loved". Unrated
            renders "pending" rather than an empty cell — a blank beside a
            filled neighbour reads as a field someone forgot. -#}
        {% if item.canon %}<span class="cell rating badge-g b-canon">canon</span>
        {% elif item.rating %}<span class="cell rating r-{{ item.rating.slug }}{% if item.rating.partial %} partial{% endif %}">{{ item.rating.title }}</span>
        {% else %}<span class="cell rating r-pending">pending</span>{% endif %}
      </span>
      <span class="shelf-caption">{{ item.title }}</span>
    </a>
  </li>
  {% endfor %}
</ul>
</div>
